---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { toolPath } from '@lib/paths';
import { toolPathBySlug } from '@lib/tools/tool-link';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = toolPath({ slug: config.slug, segment: category.segment });
const remHref = toolPathBySlug('px-to-rem-converter');
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>How do you build a modular type scale? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Enter a base size and pick a ratio. The page returns eight steps, from caption
      up to display, each in pixels and in rem. Body is the size you typed. Every
      step above body multiplies by the ratio. Every step below divides by it.
      Copy CSS takes the :root block.
    </p>
    <p>
      The variables are --type-caption, --type-small, --type-body, --type-lead,
      --type-h3, --type-h2, --type-h1, and --type-display. A 16px base makes
      --type-body equal 1rem, because rem is computed against a 16px root.
      The pixel column is the size you will see at that default root.
    </p>
    <p>
      The sample word on each row is Ag, set at the real pixel size, inside a
      box that scrolls. A 177px display step does not stretch the whole page.
      You still see the number. The arithmetic stays in the browser. Nothing
      is uploaded.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Open the Type Scale Generator →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="ratio">
    <h2>Which ratio should a font scale use? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Start with 1.25, the major third. From a 16px body the steps stay close
      enough for an interface, and display lands under 50px. Use 1.125, the
      major second, when captions and headings should barely move apart.
      Use 1.2, the minor third, when you want a little more air than 1.125
      and less jump than 1.25.
    </p>
    <p>
      1.333 is the perfect fourth, rounded from 4/3. 1.5 is the perfect fifth.
      Both are musical names for a multiplier, not a claim about music on the
      page. They suit posters and marketing pages more than a settings screen.
      The golden ratio, 1.618, is the largest preset. Major third versus golden
      ratio comes down to the page: 1.25 for an interface, 1.618 when display
      type should feel like a poster.
    </p>
    <p>
      Five of the six presets are musical intervals, and 1.25 maps to the major
      third. The golden ratio is the exception, which comes from geometry, not
      music. The names help you remember the number. They do not change the math.
      Two teams can both say "major third" and mean 1.25. If a spec writes
      1.250, that is the same preset. There is no custom field, because an
      unnamed ratio is hard to repeat later. Pick one of the six and keep
      the name next to the CSS.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, start from a 16px body. Input: base 16 and ratio 1.25, the major third.
      Output: body is 16px and 1rem, h1 is about 39.06px, and caption is about 10.24px, under
      the 12px floor, so the note names it.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="One ratio, every step">
      <p>
        Body times the ratio, repeated, is the whole scale. Change the ratio
        and every heading moves together. You do not nudge h1 by two pixels
        and leave h2 behind.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="bounds">
    <h2>When is a step too small or too large? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Caption under 12px is too small to read as text on a phone. Display over
      64px overflows a phone before the sentence does. The note names those
      ends and stays hidden when both sit inside the band. The steps are still
      printed either way. The note is a warning, not a block.
    </p>
    <p>
      A 16px base and a 1.125 ratio is the quiet case. Caption stays at or
      above 12px, and display stays at or below 64px, so you see sizes and
      CSS and no warning. Switch to 1.25 and caption falls to about 10.24px.
      The note names caption and the 12px floor. Display at that ratio is
      still a normal heading.
    </p>
    <p>
      The golden ratio on a 16px base fails both tests. Caption is near 6px.
      Display is near 177px. The note names both. That scale can still be
      what you want for a hero on a wide screen. It is a poor default for
      a phone UI. Raise the base if you need the small steps to clear 12px,
      or drop display and use h2 for the largest phone heading.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Trusting an elegant ratio on a phone">
      <p>
        A golden ratio looks even in a diagram and harsh in a 390px column.
        Read the pixel numbers, not only the sample on a wide monitor.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="rem">
    <h2>Why are the CSS variables in rem? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Rem follows the root font size, so a reader who enlarges text gets a
      larger scale. That is the difference in px sizes versus rem sizes: pixels
      would freeze the ramp at the size you copied.
      The page assumes the root is 16px, which is the browser default and
      the default on the <a href={remHref}>px to rem converter</a>. --type-body
      is then base divided by 16.
    </p>
    <p>
      If html sets font-size to 18px, every rem on this list renders larger
      than the pixel column. The pixel column did not lie. It described a
      16px root. Convert a single step on the px to rem page when your root
      is different, or divide by your root yourself. This scale will not
      guess a root you have not typed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Line height is not in the block. A useful companion is a unitless line
      height near 1.4 for body and closer to 1.1 for display. Put that next
      to the variables in your stylesheet. The generator keeps the file to
      sizes, because a line-height preset would pretend to know the font.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="note" heading="Root and base are different knobs">
      <p>
        Base is the body step you want. Root is what 1rem means in the browser.
        They match only when both are 16px. Set them on purpose.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="names">
    <h2>How should the step names be used? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Caption and small are for labels, meta text, and legal lines. Body is
      the paragraph. Lead is the sentence under a title. Then h3, h2, h1,
      and display. Use the name that matches the job, not the HTML tag you
      happened to pick. A page can have one h1 and still set it in --type-h2
      if h1 is too loud.
    </p>
    <p>
      The powers are fixed. Caption is body divided by the ratio twice.
      Display is body multiplied five times. There is no ninth step. If you
      need a size between h2 and h1, you want a smaller ratio, not a new
      name. Adding a one-off pixel value breaks the scale the next time
      someone changes the base.
    </p>
    <p>
      Keep the token names when you change the ratio. A component that
      reads --type-h2 should not be edited because you tried 1.333 instead
      of 1.25. The value moves. The name stays. That is the point of a
      variable. The copied block is the whole contract.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="preview">
    <h2>What does the sample row show? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Each row sets the word Ag at that step's pixel size. Ag shows an
      ascender and a descender, so you can see the body of the letter and
      not only a capital height. The font is whatever the page is using.
      This tool does not load a family, a weight, or a tracking value.
    </p>
    <p>
      The sample sits in a box with a maximum height. A display step larger
      than that box scrolls inside the row. The meta line under it always
      shows the label, the pixels, and the rem, so you do not have to scroll
      the sample to know the number. Copy CSS does not include the word Ag.
      It includes only the variables.
    </p>
    <p>
      On a phone the rows stack in one column. The base field and the ratio
      menu come first, so the controls sit in the first screen. The steps
      follow. Wide windows put the controls and the steps side by side.
      Nothing on the page needs a horizontal scroll of the whole window.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="choose">
    <h2>How do you choose a scale for a real page? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Write down the smallest text you will actually ship and the largest
      heading you will put on a phone. Set the base to the paragraph size.
      Move the ratio until caption clears 12px and display stays near the
      largest heading you can accept. If display is the thing that breaks,
      keep the ratio and use a lower step for that heading.
    </p>
    <p>
      Check both ends after you change the base. A larger base lifts caption
      over the floor and also inflates display. A smaller base does the
      reverse. The note is the checklist. When it disappears, both ends are
      inside the band. When it names one end, fix that end before you paste
      the CSS into a production stylesheet.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then paste the block into :root and point body at --type-body. Point
      the title at the step you chose. Leave the unused variables in the
      file. They cost nothing, and the next screen may need caption. Revisit
      the ratio when the font changes, because a wide face at 16px feels
      larger than a narrow one at the same number.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Deleting the steps you are not using yet">
      <p>
        A scale with holes gets refilled with one-off pixels. Keep the eight
        variables, and simply do not reference the ones this screen skips.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>
